Apple has canceled plans for a foldable MacBook and a larger <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/macbook-ultra/">OLED</a> <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macbook-pro/">MacBook Pro</a> model, according to research firm <a href="https://omdiaevents.com/china-display-conference/comparison-and-analysis-of-oled-processes/">Omdia</a>.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2025/10/M5-MacBook-Pro.jpg" alt="" width="1960" height="1102" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1023242" /><br/>
In a new research note, Omdia said Apple has scrapped development of a large foldable MacBook that would have measured between 16.1 and 18 inches when unfolded. It was <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/08/04/apple-larger-foldable-miss-2026-production/">previously reported</a> that this device had already slipped out of its original 2026 production window, with Omdia among the firms tracking it at the time, though under that earlier reporting it was generally described as a large-screened foldable <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad/">iPad</a> rather than a MacBook.<br/>
<br/>
Apple has also apparently called off a separate, previously planned MacBook with an &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; display between 14.4 and 16.1 inches in size, which would have sat in its own tier between the standard &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; sizes and the canceled foldable. With both of those product lines scrapped, the 14.3 to 16.3 inch size range will seemingly remain exclusive to the &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; series going forward.<br/>
<br/>
Omdia said Apple's broader strategy of bringing &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; displays to its entire &zwnj;iPad&zwnj; and MacBook lineup has not changed, but the company has reworked which products will get &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; and what specific &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; technology each one uses, a shift that lines up with the changes detailed in Apple's rumored <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/12/16/apple-product-roadmap-2026/">2026 and 2027 product roadmap</a>.<br/>
<br/>
As part of that same reshuffle, Apple is said to be adding a <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/03/apple-new-oled-macbook-between-air-pro/">new 13.8 inch MacBook</a> positioned between the LCD <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macbook-air/">MacBook Air</a> and the &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj;, using a new generation of &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; technology that includes an LTPO+ backplane, under-panel camera, and color-filter-on-encapsulation technology, among other features. That new 13.8 inch model appears to fill the gap in Apple's lineup that the now canceled 14.4 to 16.1 inch MacBook &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; would have occupied, positioned below the Pro rather than above it.<br/>
<br/>
